  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Intermittent showers & thunderstorms possible\nbetween Thursday evening and Saturday as a slow moving cold \nfront tracks through the region. \nSo far any shower and thunderstorm activity has been pretty meager \neast of the mountains early this afternoon given a subtle stable \nlayer aloft. It's a mashup of SCT-OVC cloud cover as a result of a \ncold front dropping south from Lake Erie and multiple pieces of \nshortwave energy ejecting east northeast from central WV and the \nsouth-central Ohio River Valley. Expect this activity to gradually \ndrift east and northeast this evening as the shortwave shunts \neastward and cold front drops south. The bulk of any shower activity \nshould hold off until near midnight and continue through the pre-\ndawn hours of Friday morning. Shower activity will be relatively \nlight to moderate with even a few embedded thunderstorms mixed in. \n\nThe severe weather threat appears low, but not zero given CAPE \nvalues hovering between 500-1500 j/kg and shear values around 30-40 \nkts. With a stronger piece of shortwave energy set to kick east out \nof WV expect an uptick in thunderstorm chances mainly across central \nVA and southern MD this evening where instability will be maximized \ndue to more sunshine. These areas could see spotty clusters of \nthunderstorms with gusty winds as the primary threat along with \nfrequent lightning and locally heavy rainfall. Not everyone will see \nthe rain this afternoon and evening with more scattered to \nwidespread showers expected later this evening and pre-dawn Friday \nmorning as the front drops through. \n\nHighs today will range from the mid 80s along the PA/MD line to low \n90s over southern MD and central VA (70S mountains). Lows tonight \nwill fall back into the low to mid 60s with upper 60s over central \nVA and southern MD. Some fog is possible west of the Blue Ridge \nalthough low clouds may be a bit more prevalent given the front \nnearby. \n\nAdditional rain showers could track east through the region \nFriday morning as the front sags to the south. Behind the front,\ninstability lingers as a shortwave trough builds over the Ohio \nRiver Valley and begins to move through the region. This could \nbring additional intermittent showers and thunderstorms through \nthe region late Friday and into Saturday as this system moves \nthrough. All told, total rainfall accumulation over the next \n48-72 hours could range between 0.5 inches to greater than 1 \ninch in areas that receive multiple rounds of these intermittent\nshowers/storms. \n\nHighs through the weekend range in the mid-80s across most of the \narea, with humidity remaining elevated as the cold front slowly\nmeanders to the south. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures and dry conditions expected Monday \nand Tuesday; showers possible along a cold front by the middle of \nnext week.\n\nAn upper level ridge is expected build overhead during the first \nhalf of next week supporting warmer than normal temperatures and dry \nconditions. The ridge starts breaking down during the second half of \nthe week as a shortwave-trough digs across the Great Lakes. A cold \nfront is forecast to cross the area next Thursday and will likely \nbring a few showers or possible thunderstorms, but the probability \nof severe wx remains too low.",
